# Clean Up Test Data After Feature Work

When implementing a feature and creating temporary or ad-hoc test data (seed records, fixture rows, manual DB/Firestore entries, sample files, local-only scripts, etc.), treat cleanup as part of finishing the work.

## Required workflow

1. Track every piece of test data you create during the feature (what it is, where it lives).
2. After the feature is done and the code is **committed and pushed**, remove that temporary test data.
3. Confirm nothing leftover remains in local DBs, emulators, cloud envs, fixtures, or scratch files unless it is intentional, permanent seed/fixture data meant to stay in the repo.

## Do

- Prefer isolated, clearly named test data so it is easy to find and delete
- Clean up remote/emulator data you created for manual verification, not only local files
- Leave permanent fixtures/seeds that the feature or tests intentionally depend on

## Don't

- Leave orphaned manual test records, scratch seed scripts, or throwaway sample files after push
- Commit temporary verification-only data that was never meant to ship
- Assume "tests pass" means environments are clean — clean data separately after ship

## Example

```text
❌ BAD: Add Firestore docs / DB rows to verify a feature, then commit + push and stop.

✅ GOOD: Verify with temporary data → commit + push the feature → delete the temporary
         docs/rows/files and confirm the environment is clean.
```
